\section{Expand syntax, not printed names}

Jon Pearce's \emph{Programming and Meta-Programming in Scheme} introduces macro expanders by writing procedures that construct expressions with quasiquotation. This explains how a transformer computes output, but building an expression as a list of symbols alone does not account for lexical context. The \emph{Revised$^{7}$ Report}'s \texttt{syntax-rules} preserves referential meaning for free identifiers inserted by a template and avoids capturing use-site identifiers with introduced binders. To do both, an expander must distinguish identifiers that print the same way. R.\ Kent Dybvig's \emph{The Scheme Programming Language} describes the recursive process: resolve the head as core syntax or a transformer; invoke the transformer and expand its result; or traverse those subforms that the core form designates as expressions. If the head does not name syntax, expand the form as an application.

An implementation can associate syntax with source locations and scope information. After resolving a binder, give its references the same internal identity and pass that identity to downstream turns of the compiler. Do not expand the argument of \texttt{quote} as an expression; that boundary strips lexical meaning from a datum. A \texttt{syntax-rules} literal identifier matches by binding (or by name when both identifiers are unbound), not merely by string comparison. For ellipses, the pattern match must record repetition structure: a substitution bound under two nested ellipses is a nested sequence, not one flattened list. Corresponding repeated substitutions must have compatible lengths at the depth where they are emitted.

\section{Worked expansion: two roles for one spelling}

\begin{verbatim}
(define-syntax first-true
  (syntax-rules ()
    ((_ a b) (let ((temp a)) (if temp temp b)))))
(let ((temp 7) (if 8))
  (first-true #f (+ temp if)))  ; 15
\end{verbatim}

Resolve the inner \texttt{temp} to a fresh binding and keep the \texttt{temp} in the substituted expression attached to the outer binding. The introduced \texttt{if} denotes the conditional syntax visible at the definition, whereas the final \texttt{if} in the substitution denotes an outer variable. A plain substitution of S-expressions could capture \texttt{temp}, or make the entire introduced conditional into a call to the variable \texttt{if}. Generating a fresh temporary name solves only the first problem. If the first arm is true, its value is returned after exactly one evaluation; duplicating \texttt{a} in both test and consequent would violate the behavior for effects. Test this by substituting an increment-and-read expression for \texttt{a} and an expression that would signal an error for \texttt{b}. Neither hygiene nor the pattern grammar alone checks evaluation count.

\section{Rich transformations and the phase boundary}

Dybvig's discussion of procedural \texttt{syntax-case} separates matching a syntax object from computing and assembling the replacement. It is a richer interface than R7RS-small requires. Andrew K.\ Wright's \emph{Pattern Matching for Scheme} illustrates a different use of that separation: a macro may accept patterns describing runtime data, but compiling those patterns runs at expansion time and the generated value tests run later. A variable matched by the macro expander holds syntax; a binding introduced by a generated match branch holds a runtime value. Treating them as the same storage makes both staging and diagnostics unreliable.

Matthew Flatt, Robert Bruce Findler, and Matthias Felleisen's \emph{Scheme with Classes, Mixins, and Traits} builds the class syntax using macros over procedures and a record-type generator, then builds mixins and traits using the class constructs. The macro does not need to know a superclass value at expansion time; it can generate code that works with the superclass when the class expression is evaluated. That separation permits syntactic extension without requiring arbitrary runtime expressions to be computable at expansion time.

In Racket, Krishnamurthi's \emph{Programming Languages: Application and Interpretation} uses the macro stepper to make desugaring observable without running the result. A module language with procedural transformers must decide which dependencies are available to evaluate a transformer and which will be instantiated for generated code. Loading a helper for runtime use does not automatically make it available during transformation. On the other hand, a runtime reference introduced by a macro can denote a binding in the macro's defining module. These two directions are distinct, and a dependency graph annotated with phase can distinguish them. This is a concrete instance of the dossier's staging boundary (Chapter~\ref{ch:staging}).

\section{Check the core that reaches later passes}

Sam Tobin-Hochstadt and Matthias Felleisen's \emph{The Design and Implementation of Typed Scheme} uses the macro-expanded language to avoid implementing a separate type rule for every surface extension. Some macros nonetheless impose invariants the checker cannot automatically infer. Shape-check the result first: no unresolved transformer calls should reach an ordinary expression input, and renaming a use-site binder should only change the corresponding internal identities. Then run behavioral tests for evaluation count, order, and exceptions. Retain source spans or an expansion origin chain so a type error deep in a generated conditional can point back to the user's argument. That information belongs alongside the core; recovering it from generated temporary names is too late.
