\section{Expanding a Template With Two Levels of Repetition}

Nested ellipses need a shape discipline even if every identifier
already has the right scope. Use a small schematic
\texttt{syntax-rules} transformer whose pattern accepts a list
of rows, each containing adjacent pairs of expressions. The
matcher returns two trees of captures, one for the left members
and one for the right members, both indexed by row and then
column. The template builds a list of rows, each containing
an application of a definition-site pair constructor for
each column. Implement that template step separately from the
general pattern matcher. Assume the syntax objects retain
their scopes and spans; return expanded syntax, not a list
of evaluated runtime pairs.

\begin{sudocode}
  \Input captures $A,B$ indexed by row and column
  \Output nested list syntax or incompatible shape
  \If{$|A|\ne |B|$}
    \State \Return outer shape error
  \EndIf
  \State $R\gets$ empty syntax list
  \For{$i=0$ to $|A|-1$}
    \If{$|A[i]|\ne |B[i]|$}
      \State \Return shape error at row $i$
    \EndIf
    \State $C\gets$ empty syntax list \Comment{Start a new row}
    \For{$j=0$ to $|A[i]|-1$}
      \State append $\Call{PairSyntax}{A[i][j],B[i][j]}$ to $C$
      \Comment{Preserve both substitution scopes}
    \EndFor
    \State append $\Call{ListSyntax}{C}$ to $R$
  \EndFor
  \State \Return $\Call{ListSyntax}{R}$
\end{sudocode}

For rows \verb|(((1 2) (3 4)) () ((5 6)))| the left
captures are $[[1,3],[],[5]]$ and the right captures are
$[[2,4],[],[6]]$. The first row generates two pair calls,
the second generates an empty list call, and the third
generates one pair call. With \texttt{cons} and \texttt{list}
as the definition-site constructors, evaluation yields
\verb|(((1 . 2) (3 . 4)) () ((5 . 6)))|.
Flattening each capture before zipping would still generate
three pairs but lose the empty row and the original grouping.
Uniform row lengths would make that defect harder to detect.

\Cref{fig:visual-163} shows the zip by row and column. Even the
row that emits no pair calls still emits a list call.

